WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.440
- Welcome back.

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.180
At just six years old,

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.120
our next guest became
a symbol of a movement.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.030
In 1960, in the wake of
a Supreme Court ruling,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.850
she was escorted by four Federal Marshalls

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.370
into a previously all-white school,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.050
becoming the first black student

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.260
to attend William Frantz
Elementary in New Orleans.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.520
The scene was chaos,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.100
protestors shouting and throwing things.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.740
White parents pulled
their kids out of school

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.740
and only one teacher, one,
agreed to teach the student.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.020
So for a year, she was
escorted by federal marshals

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.100
into an empty room where she
was the only kid in the class.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.190
Today, that young girl has
become a civil rights leader,

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.840
continuing the fight.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.790
And she has a new children's book

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.800
detailing her childhood experience

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.840
called "I Am Ruby Bridges".

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.342
Let's welcome Ruby Bridges, everybody.

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:54.090
(audience applauds and cheers)

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:55.759
Oh, my gosh.

00:00:55.759 --> 00:00:58.710
It is such a pleasure to meet you.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.270
I'm running this through my head.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.190
'Cause I have a six year old

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.130
and an eight year old and I
can't imagine sending them

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:05.963
into that situation.

00:01:05.963 --> 00:01:08.070
That must have been so daunting as a kid,

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.039
but maybe you were so young, you know,

00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:11.460
it hit you differently.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.160
But you say that going to
school, that first day,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.170
you knew that something special was going

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.810
like you were special
in some sense, right?

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.100
- I really did

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.070
and it was because I'd
already gone to kindergarten.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.880
So, I thought I knew what
to expect from school,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.833
but this was definitely
something much different.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.010
And I did feel special

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.160
because I remember having to take a test

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.650
and having people come over

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.070
to the house to congratulate
me for passing the test.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.660
So, that made me feel special,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.710
but not really knowing what was to come.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.530
I mean, I was accustomed to Mardi Gras

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.200
and I remember Federal Marshalls knocking

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.830
at the door and me getting
into the car with them,

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.110
escorting me to school,
but actually thinking

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.510
that it was Mardi Gras because I was six

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.510
and had no idea what was
happening at the time.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.950
- It's so powerful and it's so,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.330
I read to my kids every night

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.130
and I love always,
first of all, thank you.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.090
I love having a new book.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.490
'Cause we read the same
ones a lot of the time.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.870
So, it's nice to have that,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.930
but also it's an empowering
thing to show them

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.700
and tell them this is a true story

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.280
and like educate them from
a young age in that sense.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.240
So, thank you for that.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.940
Just as a parent reading
to your kid every night.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.940
(audience applauds)

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.940
It's so powerful and it's real.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.990
You know, they read a lot

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.880
of fantasy things or
other things that aren't

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.950
like real historically based things.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.710
So, let's talk about that teacher though.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.180
Her name was Mrs. Henry. Right?

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.100
Do you still keep in contact?

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.110
- I do, believe it or
not, she's still alive.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.960
She lives in Boston and
whenever I'm in Boston,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.270
usually every year I
get an opportunity to go

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.380
and visit with her.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.110
She was an amazing teacher

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:56.490
and not really understanding
why that crowd was outside.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:57.660
You know, I often say that,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.020
once I got inside of the
building, she showed me her art

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.600
and I knew that she looked
like the crowd outside,

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.830
but she was different.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.650
And it was because of the
love that she showed me.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.260
So, I don't think that I would have gotten

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.660
through that if it had not been for her.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.490
And the fact that she's still alive

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.920
and still my very best friend,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.410
but also still a teacher.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.090
Whenever I'm making a mistake,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.473
she's definitely there to correct me. So,

00:03:23.473 --> 00:03:24.306
- Huh.

00:03:24.306 --> 00:03:28.380
- You know, we made friends
or life at six years old.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.250
It was amazing.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.790
- Yeah. Well, it was a beautiful impact

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.920
that y'all had on each other, you know,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.120
probably a reciprocal.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.040
So, today is an important day for you.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.870
It's Ruby Bridges Walk to School Day.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.780
So, what is it and how did that happen?

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.390
- Believe it or not, in
the state of California,

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.770
there was a group of fifth graders

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.897
who they have seen my movie,

00:03:49.897 --> 00:03:52.200
read all of my books, and they thought

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.180
that I should have my own holiday.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.240
And so, they worked
together to make sure that,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.780
that happened by being in
touch with their senators.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.700
And beloved and behold, last
year it became a holiday

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:06.533
in California.

00:04:06.533 --> 00:04:09.830
So, Ruby Bridges Walk to
School Day is actually

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.930
a day my own holiday.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.860
And kids get an opportunity to
come together when they walk

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.750
into the building just
as I did back in 1960.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.690
They have an opportunity to
dialogue with one another

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.810
to make sure that they have an opportunity

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.520
to make a difference in the world.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.860
So, I am extremely proud of them

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.610
because they're young
activists and you know,

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.874
we hope that

00:04:36.874 --> 00:04:39.090
if schools haven't signed

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.670
up for Ruby Bridges Walk To
School Day that they will.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.310
So, what better way to
celebrate November the 14th?

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.736
I am extremely proud of them.

00:04:45.736 --> 00:04:46.569
(audience applauds)

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:47.730
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.668
And what better way to celebrate

00:04:49.668 --> 00:04:51.810
and immerse kids physically

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.360
into teaching them something
historically as well

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.010
instead of just reading about something

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.410
or giving them a worksheet
or something like that.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.270
Like I was that kid that
really loved to be immersed

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.970
in a situation and I
learned better like that.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.740
So, I think doing that physical act

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.008
and doing things that
really point that out, like,

00:05:07.008 --> 00:05:09.630
for kids is really important
and a really cool lesson.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.180
I hope kid, I hope schools
rather do that as well.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.430
It's really, a really a powerful thing

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.970
and a powerful tool to teach
your kids about activism.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.100
Well, up next we have
an educator who's truly

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.200
walking the walk in
Charlotte, North Carolina.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.670
He's the youngest principal

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.020
in the school's history

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.460
and he is all about making sure

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.740
his students know Ruby's story.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.020
He doesn't know you're dialed in today.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.356
So, he's gonna freak out.

00:05:32.356 --> 00:05:34.740
Are you up for surprising him?

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.150
- Absolutely.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.280
- All right everybody,
we are back and you know,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.200
I'm all about educators who go all

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.320
out to inspire kids to
dream big and work hard.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.400
Our next guest is doing

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.650
just that in his hometown of Charlotte

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.753
where he recently became
the youngest principal ever

00:05:49.753 --> 00:05:51.780
from Movement Freedom Middle School.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.002
Let's welcome principal.

00:05:53.002 --> 00:05:56.130
(audience applauds)

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.120
Congratulations.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.541
- Thank you so much.

00:05:58.541 --> 00:05:59.374
Thank you.

00:05:59.374 --> 00:06:00.840
- I have never heard of, you're 20,

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.443
you just turned 25 recently, right?

00:06:02.443 --> 00:06:04.894
- I just turned 25 a
couple weeks ago, which,

00:06:04.894 --> 00:06:06.210
- That's the youngest principal.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:07.320
- It is, yeah.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.940
It's the thing in our nation,

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.878
the average age of a
principal is 48 years old.

00:06:11.878 --> 00:06:12.711
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:12.711 --> 00:06:14.370
- And so, being a 25 year old principal

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.080
that was the thing I
used to set my children

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.910
like you can be young

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.642
and still do all the
things that you want to do.

00:06:20.642 --> 00:06:21.475
- Yeah.

00:06:21.475 --> 00:06:22.308
- You can be that principal

00:06:22.308 --> 00:06:23.970
who's out in the front with
the boombox playing music,

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.400
dancing, high fiving all the children.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.233
It's a thing.

00:06:27.233 --> 00:06:28.590
So I wanted my children
to see that my age was

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:31.080
like gonna be an excuse
to not achieve excellence.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.612
- Yeah. Oh, I love that.

00:06:32.612 --> 00:06:33.445
(audience applauds)

00:06:33.445 --> 00:06:34.560
Not use it as an excuse.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.540
So, tell us about your school.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:37.920
- Yeah, so our school,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.110
we are a charter school
network in Charlotte,

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.700
North Carolina called Movement Schools.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.960
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.670
- [Kenneth] At my school
specifically, we have 98%

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.440
of our students who are
black and brown students.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:47.280
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.150
- We also have sixth

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.180
and seventh grade students
who we love so much.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.038
We're gonna add eighth grade next year.

00:06:53.038 --> 00:06:56.520
And one thing I really love
about my school specifically is

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.440
in the nation there are only 2%

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.570
of men of color who are educators.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.520
And at my school I have a plethora

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.940
of men who show up every day
giving our students their best.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.260
- Yeah, I love that.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.100
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.914
- So you carve out special time

00:07:09.914 --> 00:07:11.580
with the kids as well, right?

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:12.658
- [Kenneth] Yes.

00:07:12.658 --> 00:07:13.491
- Okay.

00:07:13.491 --> 00:07:14.324
- So, every week we have something

00:07:14.324 --> 00:07:15.720
in all the movement schools
called community meeting.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:16.943
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:16.943 --> 00:07:18.270
- And in those times is
when the whole great levels

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.170
come together

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:20.430
and we talk about like things

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:24.390
from systemic racism,
oppression, educational things.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:25.980
And specifically this week, today

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.800
as National Ruby Bridges
Walk to School Day.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.780
So, this week at Community,
meaning we're gonna talk

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.120
about like what was the
impact that Ruby Bridges had

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.310
on education and then how can you guys

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:38.340
as young leaders to have that
same impact in your community.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.320
- [Kelly] Absolutely. Have
that fire, have that passion.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.310
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:44.310
- Well we love how you
connect with your students

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.700
and teachers on such a
deep and important level.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.830
So, we're not the only ones.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:51.270
In fact, we have someone dialed

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.310
in who wants to acknowledge
your achievements.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.233
Yep. So, say hello to Ruby Bridges.

00:07:56.756 --> 00:07:58.372
(audience applauds and cheers)

00:07:58.372 --> 00:08:00.261
- Oh my gosh.

00:08:00.261 --> 00:08:01.399
Hi.

00:08:01.399 --> 00:08:02.460
- Oh my gosh.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:03.293
- Oh my goodness.

00:08:03.293 --> 00:08:05.910
- Youngest principal ever.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.310
- I mean, is this not so impressive?

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:09.420
Kenneth is so impressive.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.340
What do you wanna say Ruby?

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:12.600
- Kenneth? I just wanna say

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.040
that it is so heartwarming
to hear you talk

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.200
about kids the way that you do,

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.600
to also have you say that,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.170
there's not enough African American men

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.880
in education today.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.880
And I applaud you for
standing up and heating

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.550
to that calling because that's
what I feel like I'm doing.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.710
I am heating to my calling.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:42.044
And to have someone as young
as you do the same thing,

00:08:42.044 --> 00:08:42.877
- Wow.

00:08:42.877 --> 00:08:45.840
- That inspires me to
continue to do my work.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:47.101
- [Kenneth] Wow.

00:08:47.101 --> 00:08:48.960
- Ruby Bridges Walk to
School Day is about that.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.730
It is about inspiring young
people to have them dialogue

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:55.530
to help them to understand

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.150
that they too can make a difference.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.560
And that's all because of you.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:08:59.852
So, thank you so much.

00:08:59.852 --> 00:09:00.685
(audience applauds)

00:09:00.685 --> 00:09:01.518
- Thank you so much.

00:09:01.518 --> 00:09:02.935
Thank you so much.

00:09:02.935 --> 00:09:03.768
- Oh my gosh.

00:09:03.768 --> 00:09:05.167
- Oh my gosh. You both are so powerful

00:09:05.167 --> 00:09:07.890
and infectious and just
like great to be around.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.810
Please come anytime.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.652
So, what do you wanna say to Ruby?

00:09:12.652 --> 00:09:13.530
- Ms. Bridges,

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.510
I just wanna say I'm thankful

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:20.510
for your boldness, your
confidence, your servitude.

00:09:21.676 --> 00:09:24.300
The thing that you did so many years ago

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:25.890
is a thing that shapes who we are

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:29.310
as a people while we can walk
and understand the importance

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:32.280
of being in an equitable
place to like really learn

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:33.690
and understand who we are.

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:34.620
But it was you walking

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.177
into that school with all
the adversity, the obstacles,

00:09:37.177 --> 00:09:39.090
the people who told
you you couldn't do it.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.560
But you did it anyway.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:41.910
And so, children seem

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.180
like this is a real person
who advocated professionally,

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.310
but also so confidently is

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:49.620
why they think they can
also do the same thing.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.870
So, I'm so appreciative
for you, for your walk,

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:54.540
your journey and just the
bonus that you've shown

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:56.430
and that you continue to show even today.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:57.745
- Oh my gosh.

00:09:57.745 --> 00:09:59.280
(audience applauds)

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.977
- Well, I was just,

00:10:00.977 --> 00:10:03.090
so that you understand,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.210
I would not have been able
to make it through that year

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:09.120
if it had not been for another teacher.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.162
- [Kenneth] Yeah.

00:10:10.162 --> 00:10:12.570
- So, I am so proud that
you stepped up to the plate

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.280
to actually teach.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.259
- Yeah.

00:10:15.259 --> 00:10:17.790
- We have to continue to do that and fight

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:20.070
so that all of our kids
have an opportunity

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:21.488
for equal education.

00:10:21.488 --> 00:10:22.321
- Absolutely.

